{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts that the cross-city nocturnal UHI gap scales with urban thermal mass and impervious surface fraction, but omits the problem's key modulator for clear, calm conditions: the sky view factor / street-canyon geometry. Under clear, calm nights, rural cooling is radiatively efficient because rural surfaces have a large sky view factor, while dense urban street canyons have a reduced sky view factor that traps outgoing longwave radiation and suppresses radiative cooling. The target's mechanism (slow release of stored sensible heat) does not explain why some cities have a larger nighttime gap than others under the specified clear, calm regime: two cities with identical impervious surface fraction and thermal mass can have very different nocturnal UHI magnitudes if one has taller, denser street canyons (lower sky view factor) and the other has low-rise sprawl. By attributing cross-city variation solely to thermal mass and impervious fraction and ignoring reduced sky view factor, the target fails the problem's requirement to identify the single best cross-city modulator.","premise":"For clear, calm nocturnal UHI, the dominant cross-city modulator of the nighttime gap is sky view factor / street-canyon geometry, not thermal mass or impervious fraction.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"}],"target":"7b9d2237bf9ff79573b33cc0f34aa976b5b348bd32d02549af8a8fb9abec5acf"}]}